Below is a **complete, production‑ready** implementation of the classic *thread‑safe* Singleton pattern in C# that uses **double‑check locking** together with **lazy initialization**.  
The code works on every .NET version (including .NET Framework 4.x, .NET Core, and .NET 5‑8) and follows the recommended guidelines from Microsoft (volatile field, `lock`, and a private constructor).

---

## 1️⃣  The Singleton class

```csharp
using System;

/// <summary>
/// A thread‑safe, lazily‑initialized Singleton.
/// </summary>
public sealed class ConfigurationManager
{
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Fields
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// The backing field that holds the singleton instance.
    /// Declared as `volatile` so that reads/writes are not reordered
    /// across the double‑check lock.
    /// </summary>
    private static volatile ConfigurationManager? _instance;

    /// <summary>
    /// An object used only for locking.  It is `readonly` so the reference
    /// cannot be changed after the type is initialized.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly object _syncRoot = new();

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Private constructor
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Prevents external code from creating instances.
    private ConfigurationManager()
    {
        // Expensive initialization can go here.
        // For demo purposes we just set a timestamp.
        CreatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Public static accessor (double‑check lock)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets the singleton instance, creating it on first use.
    /// </summary>
    public static ConfigurationManager Instance
    {
        get
        {
            // First check (no lock).  Most calls will hit this fast path.
            if (_instance is null)
            {
                // Only one thread may enter the lock block at a time.
                lock (_syncRoot)
                {
                    // Second check – another thread might have created the
                    // instance while the current thread was waiting for the lock.
                    if (_instance is null)
                    {
                        // The actual construction happens only once.
                        _instance = new ConfigurationManager();
                    }
                }
            }

            return _instance;
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Example state / behaviour
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    /// <summary>
    /// When the singleton was created (just to prove lazy init).
    /// </summary>
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Example configuration value – in a real app this could be read
    /// from a file, database, environment variables, etc.
    /// </summary>
    public string ApplicationName { get; set; } = "My Awesome App";

    /// <summary>
    /// Simulated method that would be used throughout the code base.
    /// </summary>
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[{DateTime.UtcNow:O}] [{ApplicationName}] {message}");
    }
}
```

### Why the implementation looks the way it does

| Element | Reason |
|---------|--------|
| `sealed` | Prevents inheritance, which could break the singleton guarantee. |
| `private static volatile ConfigurationManager? _instance` | `volatile` guarantees that the read of `_instance` after the lock sees the fully‑constructed object (prevents the “half‑constructed” problem on some CPUs). |
| `private static readonly object _syncRoot` | Dedicated lock object – never exposed, never changes. |
| **Double‑check** (`if (_instance is null)` before **and** after `lock`) | The first check avoids the cost of acquiring the lock on every call; the second check protects against race conditions while one thread is waiting for the lock. |
| Lazy initialization | The instance is created **only** when `Instance` is first accessed, not at type‑load time. |
| Private constructor | Guarantees that external code cannot create another instance. |
| Public members (`CreatedAt`, `ApplicationName`, `Log`) | Demonstrate that the singleton can hold state and behaviour. |

---

## 2️⃣  Using the Singleton

```csharp
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // The first call triggers lazy creation.
        ConfigurationManager.Instance.Log("Application started.");

        // Subsequent calls reuse the same instance.
        ConfigurationManager.Instance.ApplicationName = "SuperApp";
        ConfigurationManager.Instance.Log("Changed application name.");

        // Demonstrate that the instance is truly a singleton:
        var first  = ConfigurationManager.Instance;
        var second = ConfigurationManager.Instance;

        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(first, second)
            ? "Both references point to the same object."
            : "Oops – different instances!");
    }
}
```

**Sample output**

```
[2026-08-20T14:23:45.1234567Z] [My Awesome App] Application started.
[2026-08-20T14:23:45.1237890Z] [SuperApp] Changed application name.
Both references point to the same object.
```

---

## 3️⃣  Alternative: `Lazy<T>` (for completeness)

If you are on .NET 4 or later you can obtain the same guarantees *with less code* by delegating the lazy‑initialization to `System.Lazy<T>`:

```csharp
public sealed class ConfigurationManager
{
    private static readonly Lazy<ConfigurationManager> _lazy =
        new(() => new ConfigurationManager(), System.Threading.LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication);

    public static ConfigurationManager Instance => _lazy.Value;

    private ConfigurationManager()
    {
        // Expensive init here
    }

    // … same public members as before …
}
```

`Lazy<T>` internally uses a double‑check lock (or a lock‑free algorithm depending on the chosen `LazyThreadSafetyMode`).  
The explicit double‑check version shown earlier is still useful when you need **full control** over the lock object or when you are targeting older runtimes that lack `Lazy<T>`.

---

## 4️⃣  Checklist for Production Use

| ✅ Checklist Item | How to Verify |
|-------------------|---------------|
| **Thread safety** – only one instance ever created under high concurrency. | Write a stress test that spawns many parallel tasks calling `ConfigurationManager.Instance` and assert `ReferenceEquals` is always true. |
| **Lazy creation** – instance not created until first use. | Put a side‑effect (e.g., `Console.WriteLine` or a static counter) in the private constructor and ensure it runs only once and only after the first call to `Instance`. |
| **No memory leaks** – the singleton lives for the lifetime of the AppDomain, which is intentional. | Ensure you do **not** implement `IDisposable` unless you really need to release unmanaged resources at process shutdown. |
| **Sealed** – prevents subclassing that could break the contract. | Confirm the class is declared `sealed`. |
| **Serializable (optional)** – if the singleton must survive remoting or binary serialization, add a custom `GetObjectData`/`OnDeserialized` implementation that returns the existing instance. | Add `[Serializable]` and a `private ConfigurationManager(SerializationInfo, StreamingContext)` ctor that sets `_instance = this;`. |
| **Testing** – unit tests for the public members (e.g., `Log`, `ApplicationName`). | Write a simple xUnit/NUnit test that uses `ConfigurationManager.Instance` and asserts behavior. |

---

### TL;DR

* Use the **double‑check lock** pattern shown in the first code block for a classic, explicit singleton.  
* If you prefer a more concise solution and target .NET 4+, `Lazy<T>` gives you the same guarantees with less boilerplate.  

Either way, you now have a **thread‑safe, lazily‑initialized Singleton** ready for production code. Happy coding!
